{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 4/5","reason":"Each nonzero residue modulo 5 has fourth power 1 and hence sixteenth power 1; multiples of 5 give remainder 0. There are 2020/5 = 404 multiples of 5, so the probability is (2020 - 404)/2020 = 4/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"units-digit-squaring","label":"Track the units digit","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-squaring":["math:modular-powers","cnm_285"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_285"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 505","reason":"Modulo 101, 10²≡-1 and 10⁴≡1, with powers cycling through 1, 10, -1, -10. Thus the nth term is divisible by 101 exactly when n≡1 mod 4. Among indices 1 through 2018, these are 1,5,...,2017, totaling 505.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"split-exponents-by-parity","label":"Use alternating power remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"split-exponents-by-parity":["math:modular-powers","cnm_030"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_030"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2^607 - 1","reason":"For choice C, the residues modulo 2, 3, 5, and 7 are respectively 1, 1, 2, and 1, all nonzero. The other choices have small prime divisors: A and D are divisible by 3, while B and E are divisible by 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"test-the-small-primes","label":"Check the short power cycles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"test-the-small-primes":["math:modular-powers","cnm_p157"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 7","reason":"Powers of both 2 and 3 have units-digit cycles of length 4. Since 2023≡3 and 2022≡2 modulo 4, the two units digits are 8 and 9. Their sum has units digit 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"units-digit-cycles","label":"Follow the repeating digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycles":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (4)","reason":"We have 8P(m) = 4m + 2m^2 + m^4 + m^8. For even m every term is divisible by 8. For odd m, m^2 ≡ m^4 ≡ m^8 ≡ 1 modulo 8, so the numerator is congruent to 4 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 8. Thus all four values are integers.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"check-residues-modulo-eight","label":"Check remainders modulo 8","methodDirectNodeIds":{"check-residues-modulo-eight":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (2 remainders)","reason":"If 5 divides a, then 125 divides a¹⁰⁰, giving remainder 0. Otherwise gcd(a,125)=1, and Euler's theorem with φ(125)=125-25=100 gives a¹⁰⁰ ≡ 1 modulo 125. Both remainders occur, for example at a=0 and a=1, so there are exactly two.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"binomial-remainder-classes","label":"Use a binomial expansion","methodDirectNodeIds":{"binomial-remainder-classes":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h240"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h240"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (5)","reason":"For exponents 2 through 6, the residues of powers of 6 modulo 100 are 36,16,96,76,56, and this five-step cycle repeats. Since 6^6 = 46656 ≡ 1 modulo 5, the last two digits are 56, giving tens digit 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"last-two-digit-cycle","label":"Track the last two digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"last-two-digit-cycle":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remainders-modulo-four-and-twenty-five","label":"Combine two smaller remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remainders-modulo-four-and-twenty-five":["math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 7","reason":"Powers of 7 have units digits 7, 9, 3, 1 in a cycle of length 4. Since 753≡1 mod 4, the required digit is 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_019"],"id":"units-digit-cycle","label":"Follow the units-digit cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycle":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_019"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 9","reason":"Modulo 10, 13≡3, so the product is congruent to 3^2004·7^1002. The first factor is 1 and the second is 9, giving units digit 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_143"],"id":"units-digit-cycles","label":"Track units-digit cycles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycles":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 8","reason":"Both bases are congruent to -1 modulo 10 and both exponents are odd. The sum is therefore congruent to -2, or 8, modulo 10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2000_AMC_8_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_019","cnm_p156"],"id":"alternate-units-digits","label":"Follow the units-digit cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"alternate-units-digits":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_019","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4","reason":"Since 3^2 is congruent to 1 modulo 8, each pair 3^(2k)+3^(2k+1) is congruent to 4. There are 1005 pairs, so the sum is congruent to 4020, whose remainder modulo 8 is 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_164"],"id":"pair-repeating-remainders","label":"Pair the repeating remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-repeating-remainders":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_164"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 1","reason":"The units digits of successive powers of 13 repeat as 3, 9, 7, 1. Since 2012 = 4*503, the required units digit is 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_8_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_019","cnm_p017"],"id":"units-digit-cycle","label":"Follow the units-digit cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycle":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_019","cnm_p017"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (2/5)","reason":"For m=11,13,15,17,19, the numbers of favorable exponents are respectively 20,5,0,5,10. The cycles for 13 and 17 require n divisible by 4, and 19 requires even n. Thus the probability is 40/(5·20)=2/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"units-digit-cycle-average","label":"Average the probabilities from units digit cycles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycle-average":["math:modular-powers","cnm_285","cnm_h246"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_285","cnm_h246"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 4","reason":"For every integer a, a³-a=(a-1)a(a+1) is divisible by 6. Therefore the requested sum is congruent to 2018^2018≡2^2018≡4 mod 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cube-congruence-and-power-cycle","label":"Reduce cubes and use a power cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-congruence-and-power-cycle":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, remainder 3","reason":"N=2·9^10+7·9^9+6·9^6+5·9+2. Modulo 5 this is 2-7+6-5+2=-2≡3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"alternate-power-remainders","label":"Use alternating remainders of powers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"alternate-power-remainders":["cnm_024","math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 2^607 - 1","reason":"The residues of 2^607 - 1 modulo 2, 3, 5, and 7 are respectively 1, 1, 2, and 1. Meanwhile, A is divisible by 3, B by 5, D by 3, and E by 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"test-the-small-primes","label":"Check the short power cycles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"test-the-small-primes":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p157"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5","reason":"Let S_n be the nth row sum. Then S_1=1 and S_n=2S_(n-1)+n-2, giving S_n=2^n-n. Since 2023 is congruent to 3 modulo 4, 2^2023 has units digit 8. Thus S_2023 has units digit 8-3=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"row-sum-recurrence","label":"Make the row sums double","methodDirectNodeIds":{"row-sum-recurrence":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","math:modular-powers"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"shift-to-pascal-triangle","label":"Reveal Pascal's triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-to-pascal-triangle":["cnm_h243","cnm_h208","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h243","cnm_h208","math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2","reason":"If 5 divides a, then a^100 is divisible by 125 and has remainder 0. Otherwise gcd(a,125) = 1, and Euler's theorem gives a^100 ≡ 1 modulo 125 because φ(125) = 125-25 = 100. Both remainders occur, for example at a = 5 and a = 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-and-expand","label":"Square first, then expand","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-expand":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h240","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h240","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3","reason":"Since 2^6 = 64 ≡ -1 modulo 13 and 1000 = 6·166 + 4, we obtain 2^1000 ≡ (-1)^166·16 ≡ 3 modulo 13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_142","cnm_143"],"id":"sixth-power-reduction","label":"Use a small signed remainder","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sixth-power-reduction":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_142","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 1","reason":"1999 is congruent to -1 modulo 5, so 1999^2000 is congruent to (-1)^2000 = 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_8_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_019","cnm_p115"],"id":"pair-ones-digits","label":"Pair factors and track the ones digit","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-ones-digits":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_019","cnm_p115"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 7","reason":"Powers of 13 have units digits 3,9,7,1 with period 4. Since 2003≡3 mod 4, the required units digit is 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_10A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p017","cnm_p050","cnm_019"],"id":"units-digit-cycle","label":"Track the repeating units digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycle":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_p017","cnm_p050","cnm_019"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 6","reason":"Modulo 10, 2008^2 is 4 and 2^2008 is 6, so k ends in 0 and k^2 ends in 0. Also k is divisible by 4. The cycle 2, 4, 8, 6 therefore makes 2^k end in 6, giving final units digit 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_019"],"id":"units-digit-cycle","label":"Use the four-step cycle of powers of two","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycle":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p153","cnm_019"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 6","reason":"Modulo 10, 2008^2 ≡ 4 and 2^2008 ≡ 6, so k ≡ 0 and k^2 ≡ 0. Also k ≡ 0 modulo 4, so 2^k has units digit 6. The requested units digit is therefore 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_020"],"id":"units-digit-cycle","label":"Track the four-digit cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycle":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p153","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (4)","reason":"7^4 = 2401 is congruent to 1 modulo 100. Since 2011 = 4·502 + 3, the last two digits equal those of 7^3 = 343, namely 43. The tens digit is 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_8_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_019","cnm_p017","cnm_p050"],"id":"last-two-digit-cycle","label":"Track the last two digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"last-two-digit-cycle":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_019","cnm_p017","cnm_p050"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (0)","reason":"Modulo 100, 15^2 ≡ 25 and every positive power of 25 is congruent to 25. Thus 2015^2016 - 2017 ≡ 25 - 17 = 8. Its final two digits are 08, so the tens digit is 0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_p017","cnm_143"],"id":"last-two-digits","label":"Track only the last two digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"last-two-digits":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p017","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3/16","reason":"Both cycles have length four, with each residue class represented 25 times. The digit pairs summing to 8 modulo 10 are (1,7), (7,1), and (9,9), giving three of sixteen equally likely pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1990_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_h231","cnm_309"],"id":"units-digit-cycles","label":"Count pairs of units digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"units-digit-cycles":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h137"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h137","math:modular-congruence","cnm_h231","cnm_309"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5","reason":"For fixed n, each of the 10 elements has n+1 first-appearance options, so K=∑ from k=2 to 11 of k^10. For every integer k, k^10 is congruent to k² modulo 10. The ten bases cover all residues, so K is congruent to ∑ from r=0 to 9 of r²=285, giving remainder 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"first-appearance-encoding","label":"Record when each element appears","methodDirectNodeIds":{"first-appearance-encoding":["math:bijective-counting","math:modular-powers","cnm_h006","cnm_h231","cnm_h230"]},"nodeIds":["math:bijective-counting","math:modular-powers","cnm_h006","cnm_h231","cnm_h230"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"first-set-recurrence","label":"Count by the first set","methodDirectNodeIds":{"first-set-recurrence":["math:recursive-counting-probability","cnm_h237","cnm_h240","math:modular-powers","cnm_h006"]},"nodeIds":["math:recursive-counting-probability","cnm_h237","cnm_h240","math:modular-powers","cnm_h006"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 74","reason":"The row sums satisfy f(n)=2f(n−1)+2 with f(1)=0, hence f(n)=2^n−2. Since 2^20≡76 modulo 100 and 76²≡76, 2^100≡76, giving f(100)≡74.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h197","cnm_h207","math:modular-congruence"],"id":"1995_AMC_12_Q27_m1","label":"Track the row sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1995_AMC_12_Q27_m1":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","math:modular-powers","cnm_h197","cnm_h207","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 6","reason":"Modulo 1000, 2011^2011 ≡ (1+10)^2011 ≡ 1 + 10·2011 + 100·C(2011,2). Since C(2011,2) = 2021055, the residue is 611, whose hundreds digit is 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"residue-power-cycle","label":"Use a residue power cycle","nodeIds":["math:modular-powers"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_142","cnm_143","cnm_p053"],"id":"solution:residue-power-cycle:c2b9e8adc736","label":"Find a repeating block of powers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"residue-power-cycle":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:modular-congruence","cnm_142","cnm_143","cnm_p053"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 30","reason":"For every integer n, n^5-n is divisible by 2, 3, and 5, hence by 30. At n=2 the value is exactly 30, so no larger positive integer can divide every term.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-modulus-powers","label":"Use power congruences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-modulus-powers":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 63 and 65","reason":"Because 12 divides 48, 2^12-1 divides 2^48-1. Also 2^12-1 = (64-1)(64+1) = 63*65, so both proposed numbers divide the given integer.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"power-residues","label":"Check power residues","methodDirectNodeIds":{"power-residues":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 2^1005","reason":"The expression is 2^1002(25^501-1)=2^1002·24·(1+25+...+25^500). The sum contains 501 odd terms and is odd, so exactly three additional factors of 2 occur. The greatest power is therefore 2^1005.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_10B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"modulo-sixteen","label":"Power residues modulo 16","methodDirectNodeIds":{"modulo-sixteen":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_143","cnm_151","cnm_142","cnm_p156","math:modular-congruence"],"id":"remainders-modulo-sixteen","label":"Find the remainder after division by 16","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remainders-modulo-sixteen":["math:prime-factorization","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:modular-powers","cnm_143","cnm_151","cnm_142","cnm_p156","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 7","reason":"Let n = k+2 ≥ 3. For n = 3, 4, 5, factoring out 2^n leaves an odd factor, so N(k) = n. For n ≥ 7, factoring out 64 leaves an odd factor, so N(k) = 6. At n = 6, I_k = 64(5^6+1) = 128 × 7813, giving N(4) = 7. Hence the maximum is 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_10A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"modular-divisibility-tests","label":"Check residues modulo powers of 2","methodDirectNodeIds":{"modular-divisibility-tests":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 201","reason":"Let M=2^101+2^51+1. Then M(2^101-2^51+1)=2^202+1, so 2^202+202=M(2^101-2^51+1)+201. Since 0≤201<M, the remainder is 201.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-and-reduce","label":"Square and reduce a congruence","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-reduce":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_148","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 6","reason":"Write 7Sₙ=1+aₙ2ⁿ. Then a₁=3 and aₙ₊₁=(aₙ+7xₙ)/2. Requiring this to be integral produces the repeating carry cycle 3,5,6 and the repeating digits x₁,x₂,x₃=1,1,0. Consequently x₂₀₁₉,x₂₀₂₀,x₂₀₂₁,x₂₀₂₂ are 0,1,1,0, giving 0+2+4+0=6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"power-cycle-prefix","label":"Recover a prefix using powers modulo 7","methodDirectNodeIds":{"power-cycle-prefix":["math:modular-powers","math:floor-ceiling"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:floor-ceiling"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prefixes-modulo-seven","label":"Find the two partial sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prefixes-modulo-seven":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_024"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (x^6+x^3+1)","reason":"Set y=x³, so P=y^674+y^337+1. Modulo y²+y+1, we have y³=1. Since 337≡1 modulo 3, the remainder is y²+y+1=0. Therefore x⁶+x³+1 divides P.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"integer-evaluation-elimination","label":"Eliminate by integer substitutions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"integer-evaluation-elimination":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 6","reason":"First x₀ = 1. Let qₙ = (7Sₙ - 1)/2^n. Then qₙ₊₁ = (qₙ + 7xₙ)/2, so xₙ must have the parity of qₙ. Starting at q₁ = 3 gives the repeating carry cycle 3, 5, 6 and digits x₁,x₂,x₃ = 1,1,0. Thus x₂₀₁₉,x₂₀₂₀,x₂₀₂₁,x₂₀₂₂ = 0,1,1,0, giving 0 + 2 + 4 + 0 = 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prefixes-modulo-seven","label":"Find the two partial sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prefixes-modulo-seven":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_024"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 0","reason":"The sum factors as 7^2024(1+7+49)=57·7^2024. Since 57=3·19, the remainder upon division by 19 is 0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-period-three","label":"Use the power cycle modulo 19","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-period-three":["math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 12","reason":"Let N = 90!/10²¹, since v5(90!) = 18 + 3 = 21. Also v2(90!) = 86, so N ≡ 0 modulo 4. Define U(m) as the product of integers up to m not divisible by 5. Modulo 25, U(90) = 1, U(18) = 4, and U(3) = 6. Thus N ≡ U(90)U(18)U(3)·2⁻²¹ ≡ 24·13 ≡ 12 modulo 25. Combining the two residues gives N ≡ 12 modulo 100.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues","label":"Remove zeros and combine residues","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"remove-zeros-and-find-remainders","label":"Remove the zeros and find small remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-zeros-and-find-remainders":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 12","reason":"The valuations are v₅(90!)=21 and v₂(90!)=86. Let U(N)=N!/5^{v₅(N!)}. Grouping factors not divisible by 5 gives U(90)≡1·4·6=24 modulo 25. Thus M=90!/10²¹ satisfies M≡24(2²¹)⁻¹≡12 modulo 25. Also v₂(M)=65, so M≡0 modulo 4. These conditions give M≡12 modulo 100.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues","label":"Remove zeros and combine residues","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"remove-fives-and-find-remainders","label":"Remove trailing zeros and track remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-fives-and-find-remainders":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (9)","reason":"Modulo 9, concatenation gives N ≡ 1+2+...+44 = 990 ≡ 0. Its last digit gives N ≡ 4 modulo 5. The unique residue modulo 45 satisfying both conditions is 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reduce-concatenation-powers","label":"Reduce powers of 10 modulo 45","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduce-concatenation-powers":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h204"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","cnm_h204"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 17","reason":"The exponents satisfy b_0=0, b_1=1 and b_n=b_(n−1)+2b_(n−2), giving b_n=(2^n−(−1)^n)/3. Their sum is S_k=(2^(k+2)−3−(−1)^k)/6. The order of 2 modulo 19 is 18, so 19 divides S_k first at odd k=17 or even k=18. Therefore the smallest is 17; indeed S_17=87381=19·4599, making the product 2^4599.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"closed-form-power-cycle","label":"Closed form and power cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"closed-form-power-cycle":["cnm_h199","cnm_h210","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_h210","math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A","reason":"Let k=(p-1)/2, a positive integer. Since p-1 is congruent to 1 modulo p-2, (p-1)^k-1 is divisible by p-2. Equivalently, it equals (p-2)(1+(p-1)+...+(p-1)^(k-1)).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_019","cnm_146"],"id":"remainder-one-products","label":"Track the remainder of the power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remainder-one-products":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_019","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (883)","reason":"Since 100^j≡1+99j modulo 99², the order of 100 is 99. A decimal period must be even, as 10≡-1 modulo 11, so its minimal length is 198. The repeating block consists of the two-digit blocks 00,01,...,97,99, as verified by multiplying its integer value by 9801. The digit sum is therefore 900-(9+8)=883.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p050","cnm_p120","math:modular-congruence"],"id":"two-digit-long-division","label":"Divide in two-digit blocks","methodDirectNodeIds":{"two-digit-long-division":["math:rational-decimal-periods","math:repeating-decimal-fractions","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:rational-decimal-periods","math:repeating-decimal-fractions","math:modular-powers","cnm_p050","cnm_p120","math:modular-congruence"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:modular-powers","status":"mapped","total":44}
